From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 14:05:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dsi: give the command DMA a window to start in

dsi_ctrl_enable() sets DSI_TRIG_CTRL_BLOCK_DMA_WITHIN_FRAME on 6G v1.2
and later, which asks the controller not to begin a command DMA in the
middle of a frame. The window it may use instead is programmed in a
separate register, and this driver never writes it, so it stays at
reset. The controller is told what not to do and never told when it may.

On a command mode panel the result is that a DCS write lands on top of
the pixel stream. Every occurrence reads the same:

  fifo=88881010 timeout=00000001 ack=00000000 status0=00000005
  phy=00088888 clk=008037c3 mdp_busy=1 dma_busy=0

DLN0..3_HS_FIFO_UNDERFLOW on all four lanes with the MDP mid-transfer.

Measured on a Motorola rhodep, 1080x2400 command mode with DSC, over
ninety seconds of use:

  scrolling a long list, no DCS traffic ........  0 errors
  brightness moving, screen otherwise still ...  0 errors
  brightness moving while the screen repaints .. 20 errors, all of them
                                                 within 300ms of a
                                                 backlight write

Neither half raises anything alone. Synthetic loads agree: a 60 Hz
scroll raised nothing across sixteen runs, and driving the backlight at
20, 60, 120 and 240 Hz against a still screen raised nothing either.

Two things are needed, and a first attempt that did only half of the
first changed nothing, 23 errors against 20:

  - The window has to be programmed ahead of each transfer while the
    display is up, not once when the host is enabled.

  - DSI_TRIG_CTRL_DMA_TRIGGER is declared three bits wide here with an
    enum that stops at 6, and dsi_ctrl_enable() writes TRIGGER_SW.
    Downstream clears four bits and writes 0xc for a scheduled DMA, so
    the field is wider than this map says and the value cannot be
    spelled with the enum.

The window is defined against the tear signal, so it only exists once
frames are running. Arming a transfer before that leaves the controller
waiting for something that never arrives: an attempt without any check
timed out on the first initialisation command,

  dsi_cmds2buf_tx: cmd dma tx failed, type=0x39, data0=0xf0, len=12,
                   ret=-110
  panel: sending dcs data f0 55 aa 52 08 00 failed: -110
  panel: Failed to initialize panel: -110

and the panel never lit. That the transfer blocked at all is what says
the offset and the trigger value are right, since writing either to the
wrong place changed nothing at all.

Downstream gates this on its display being enabled, but the equivalent
flag here is set too early: this panel sets prepare_prev_first, so
msm_dsi_host_enable() runs before the panel is prepared and the
initialisation commands still go out with the host marked enabled. Gate
on the MDP having completed a frame instead, which is the point the tear
signal is actually running. DSI_IRQ_CMD_MDP_DONE is already unmasked in
command mode and the interrupt handler ignores it.

The window opens TEARCHECK_WINDOW_SIZE lines past the active region and
is as wide as the active region, which is what downstream computes for
command mode. The schedule register is not in the map these headers come
from either; the offset carries the same four byte shift as the rest.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -185,6 +185,12 @@
 	bool registered;
 	bool power_on;
 	bool enabled;
+	/*
+	 * Set once the MDP has completed a frame, which is the point from
+	 * which the tear signal is running and a scheduled command DMA has a
+	 * window to land in.
+	 */
+	bool mdp_frame_seen;
 	int irq;
 };
 
@@ -805,6 +811,27 @@
 	dsi_write(msm_host, REG_DSI_CTRL, 0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Command mode DMA scheduling, as the vendor driver programs it on controller
+ * versions 2.2 through 2.5. This SoC is 2.4.
+ *
+ * REG_DSI_DMA_SCHEDULE_CTRL2 is not in the register map these headers are
+ * generated from. Downstream calls it DSI_DMA_SCHEDULE_CTRL2 at 0x104, four
+ * bytes above the mainline numbering, which is the shift the whole map
+ * carries -- DSI_TRIG_CTRL is 0x84 downstream and 0x80 here.
+ *
+ * DSI_TRIG_CTRL_DMA_TRIGGER is declared three bits wide above, with an enum
+ * that stops at 6. Downstream clears four bits and writes 0xc, so the field is
+ * wider than this map says and the value it wants cannot be spelled with the
+ * enum.
+ */
+#define REG_DSI_DMA_SCHEDULE_CTRL2	0x00000100
+#define DSI_TRIG_CTRL_DMA_TRIGGER_WIDE__MASK	0x0000000f
+#define DSI_TRIG_CTRL_DMA_TRIGGER_SCHEDULED	0x0000000c
+/* Lines past the active region before the window opens; TEARCHECK_WINDOW_SIZE
+ * downstream. */
+#define DSI_DMA_SCHEDULE_TE_MARGIN	5
+
 static bool msm_dsi_host_version_geq(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host,
 				    u32 major, u32 minor)
 {
@@ -1455,6 +1482,53 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * BLOCK_DMA_WITHIN_FRAME, set in dsi_ctrl_enable(), asks the controller to
+ * hold a command DMA back rather than let it cut into a frame. The window it
+ * may use instead is programmed here. Without it the register sits at reset
+ * and the request has nowhere to land, so a DCS write goes out on top of the
+ * pixel stream and the lane FIFOs run dry.
+ *
+ * Downstream reprograms this ahead of every transfer while the display is up,
+ * rather than once when the host is enabled, and resets the trigger controls
+ * afterwards; follow that.
+ */
+static void dsi_cmd_dma_schedule(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host)
+{
+	u32 vactive, data;
+
+	if (msm_host->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO)
+		return;
+	if (!msm_host->mode)
+		return;
+	/*
+	 * The window is defined against the tear signal, so it only exists
+	 * once frames are running. Arming a transfer before that leaves the
+	 * controller waiting for something that never arrives, and the panel's
+	 * own initialisation is the first thing to be lost.
+	 *
+	 * msm_host->enabled is not the right test: this panel sets
+	 * prepare_prev_first, so msm_dsi_host_enable() has already run by the
+	 * time the initialisation commands are sent. Wait for the MDP to have
+	 * finished a frame instead.
+	 */
+	if (!msm_host->mdp_frame_seen)
+		return;
+	if (!msm_dsi_host_version_geq(msm_host, MSM_DSI_VER_MAJOR_6G,
+				      MSM_DSI_6G_VER_MINOR_V2_2_1))
+		return;
+
+	vactive = msm_host->mode->vdisplay;
+
+	data = dsi_read(msm_host, REG_DSI_TRIG_CTRL);
+	data &= ~DSI_TRIG_CTRL_DMA_TRIGGER_WIDE__MASK;
+	data |= DSI_TRIG_CTRL_DMA_TRIGGER_SCHEDULED;
+	dsi_write(msm_host, REG_DSI_TRIG_CTRL, data);
+
+	dsi_write(msm_host, REG_DSI_DMA_SCHEDULE_CTRL2,
+		  (vactive + DSI_DMA_SCHEDULE_TE_MARGIN) | (vactive << 16));
+}
+
 static int dsi_cmd_dma_tx(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host, int len)
 {
 	const struct msm_dsi_cfg_handler *cfg_hnd = msm_host->cfg_hnd;
@@ -1470,6 +1544,8 @@
 
 	reinit_completion(&msm_host->dma_comp);
 
+	dsi_cmd_dma_schedule(msm_host);
+
 	dsi_wait4video_eng_busy(msm_host);
 
 	triggered = msm_dsi_manager_cmd_xfer_trigger(
@@ -1719,6 +1795,9 @@
 	if (isr & DSI_IRQ_VIDEO_DONE)
 		complete(&msm_host->video_comp);
 
+	if (isr & DSI_IRQ_CMD_MDP_DONE)
+		msm_host->mdp_frame_seen = true;
+
 	if (isr & DSI_IRQ_CMD_DMA_DONE)
 		complete(&msm_host->dma_comp);
 
@@ -2470,6 +2549,7 @@
 	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host = to_msm_dsi_host(host);
 
 	msm_host->enabled = false;
+	msm_host->mdp_frame_seen = false;
 	dsi_op_mode_config(msm_host,
 		!!(msm_host->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO), false);
 
